# DBW OPENING LINES SKILL

## INPUTS REQUIRED
Contact first name.
Name drop 1: PWD name from REED intel — use this first
if found. It is the most powerful name drop because
it implies a relationship with the government official
overseeing the work in that market.
Name drop 2: Competing bidder name from the same
project — implies you work with their competition,
which creates urgency.
Name drop 3: Local supplier name — use as fallback
when no project intel is available.

Use the best 2 to 3 name drops available. Never
fabricate names under any circumstance. If nothing
is found for a name drop position, skip it rather
than inventing something.

## NAME DROP ASSEMBLY
Build the name drop phrase using this structure:
PWD name plus "over at" plus city plus "public works,
and guys like" plus bidder name plus "and" plus
supplier name.
If no PWD is found, use bidder and supplier only.
If no project is found, use supplier names only.

## VERSION 1 — AGGRESSIVE
Use when the contact sounds skeptical, alpha, or
guarded on the call. This version challenges their
identity as a professional and forces them to align
with being sharp rather than cheap.

Script:
[FIRST NAME], what's going on — real quick, I'm
working with [NAME DROPS] right there in your area.
We've been saving those guys serious time and money
on their cuts. When your name came up, nobody hyped
you up — they just said you're a pro who knows
exactly what he's doing. So let me ask you straight
— you're not the type of guy who's okay wasting time
or burning through cheap blades just to save a few
bucks upfront right?
Good. Give me 15 seconds — I'll show you exactly
what we're doing for those guys. If it makes sense,
we'll talk. If not, I'm out of your hair. Fair?

## VERSION 2 — LAID-BACK MIDWEST
Use when the contact sounds relaxed, friendly, or
chatty. This version feels human and conversational,
like a guy leaning on a truck talking. Midwestern
contractors buy from people they like, not people
who bulldoze them.

Script:
Hey [FIRST NAME], how's it going man? Listen, I've
been working with a few guys around your area —
[NAME DROPS]. Good people. We've been having really
solid luck helping them out on their jobs, saving them
a little time, a little money. When I heard your name,
they didn't oversell it — just said you're a good,
reputable contractor who takes pride in your work.
So I figured I'd give you a quick shout. If you're
open to it, give me like 15 seconds — I'll run it
by you. If it's not for you, no hard feelings, I'll
let you get back to it. Sound fair?

## VERSION 3 — DECISION-MAKER SHORTCUT
Use when the contact answers fast, sounds busy, or
seems rushed. This version respects their time,
gets straight to the point, and ends with a
yes-question that is almost impossible to say no to.

Script:
[FIRST NAME], quick one — I work with [NAME DROPS]
in your area. We've been helping them cut faster and
get more life out of their blades. I got your name
as someone who runs a tight job.
Let me ask you this — if I could show you a better
blade that saves you time and holds up longer, you'd
at least take a look, right?
Perfect. Give me 15 seconds — I'll get straight to
the point. If it's not a fit, I'll let you go.

## FIELD READ LOGIC
After building all three versions, output a single
sentence field read recommendation for the rep.
Answered fast or sounded rushed equals Version 3.
Sounded chill, talkative, or friendly equals Version 2.
Sounded skeptical, guarded, or alpha equals Version 1.
If no personality intel is available, default to
Version 2 as the safest general approach.

## OUTPUT FORMAT
Return all three versions fully written out with the
actual contact name and name drops substituted in.
Never return template placeholders in the output.
Label them V1 AGGRESSIVE, V2 MIDWEST, and V3 SHORTCUT.
Follow with FIELD READ: and the one-sentence
recommendation.